$DIR/main.p:28:2: keyboard is an interrupt handler, so it must not start a task
    Interrupt handlers run on top of whatever code the CPU was executing.
    Starting a task allocates its stack and takes the scheduler's locks, which
    the interrupted code may hold, and then the CPU stops.
    keyboard calls rt.Go (main.p:28), which starts a task.
    A kernel that waits in an interrupt handler hangs, often only under load and
    far from the cause, so Plutonium checks the whole program at compile time
    instead.
    To hand work to normal code, use a non-blocking send:
        select { case work <- item: default: }
    (see "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10)
    (run "pluto explain E2585" for a longer explanation)
